<h2>Three ideas</h2>
<ul class="pillars principles">
  <li><strong>Software should be free.</strong> Anyone can use, copy, change, share and sell it, and its future doesn't depend on whoever wrote it first. You can't pirate what belongs to everyone.</li>
  <li><strong>Stewardship should be rewarded.</strong> The people who look after software, its stewards, should earn a good living by keeping software reliable, secure and supported, and by answering for it. Not by counting hours or locking anyone in.</li>
  <li><strong>Human attention is precious.</strong> Software should give people their time back, not track them, sell their focus or keep them hooked. Stewards are paid for their attention so that yours isn't needed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>So how does anyone get paid?</h2>
<p>The code is free, so anyone can run it themselves. Many people would rather not. They could pay a <strong>steward</strong> to host it, update it, fix it and stand behind it, typically for a flat monthly fee with a promised level of service. If the steward does a poor job, customers can take their data and leave, to another steward or to running it themselves. The aim is a business where the only way to keep customers is to deserve them.</p>
<p>AI makes the work faster. If stewards are paid for results instead of hours, faster work could mean a better living, not a smaller invoice.</p>
<p><strong>We're not against profit. We're against manufacturing dependence to extract it.</strong> <a href="/stewardship/">Read how the stewardship economy could work</a>.</p>

<h2>Not proven yet</h2>
<p>This is an idea, not a proven business model. Our next step is a small, real pilot: one steward, one free application, five to ten paying customers, and published results whether it works or not. <strong>It hasn't started.</strong> <a href="/pilot/">Read the pilot plan</a>, offer to take part, or <a href="/debate/">tell us where we're wrong</a>.</p>

<h2>Could your software survive without you?</h2>
<p>If the code is truly free, someone else should be able to pick it up and carry on. <a href="/challenge/">The challenge</a> asks anyone to try that with this project. Tools, standards and experiments that help put these ideas into practice are on <a href="/projects/">the projects page</a>, and none of them is required to take part.</p>

